This repository is published so that the results in README.md can be checked and
the engineering behind them can be read. That is its whole purpose, and reading
it is explicitly welcome.

It is not open source. No licence is granted to use, copy, modify, merge,
publish, distribute, sublicense or sell this software, or works derived from it,
in whole or in part. The absence of a permissive licence here is deliberate
rather than an oversight.

Two things are permitted beyond reading. Reproducing the measurements by running
the evaluation commands against your own vendor accounts and the benchmark's own
containers, which is the point of publishing them. And the forking that GitHub's
terms of service already extend to anyone who can see a public repository.

If you want to use any of this for anything else, please ask first by opening an
issue on this repository. The answer is likely to be yes.

Third-party dependencies keep their own licences, which are recorded in
pyproject.toml and resolved in uv.lock. The benchmark data and the public
repositories this is measured against keep theirs, which are recorded in
docs/boundary.md. Nothing above applies to them.
